WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.560
Every evening, 30-year-old Adesh sits in this
spot, watching television.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.890
This is not his home – he does not have
one.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:13.610
But Delhi’s cold and polluted winter nights
are a bit easier to bear in this government

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.280
shelter for the homeless.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.790
"It is comfortable here.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.400
I come here, watch TV, fall asleep.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.560
I came to Delhi because I can earn more here.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.119
I can work hard all day in the village, but
earn very little."

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:38.320
Adesh first came to Delhi when he was just
14 years old, to try to earn money for his

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.820
father’s tuberculosis treatment.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:45.649
Now, he is the sole wage earner in his family
of three, making 2 to 3 dollars a day working

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:47.530
odd jobs on construction sites.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:52.460
As the night wears on, the shelter starts
filling up with migrant laborers like Adesh.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.630
Some spots still lie empty.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:59.660
But this does not mean there are no more people
out in the cold.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.129
As temperatures dip and the city passes them
by, thousands of homeless people in Delhi

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:09.689
try to find a corner to call home for the
night.

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:15.350
Just a 5 minute walk from Adesh’s shelter,
this bridge is a popular spot.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.649
A sealed off portion under it is a home for
many families.

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:24.200
50 year old Lallan has been coming to Delhi
for two decades.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.319
The youngest of his six children lives with
him here.

00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:31.359
In the day, the shelter nearby serves as a
makeshift school for her.

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:37.420
But she and her mother cannot spend the night
there, as the shelter is only for men.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.779
"Alcoholics, addicts stay in the shelters.

00:01:41.779 --> 00:01:43.249
They fight, they do other things.

00:01:43.249 --> 00:01:47.249
How can I take my little children there?

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:52.049
We don’t have a home to live with our kids,
to educate them, keep them clean.

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:54.329
Where is our place?

00:01:54.329 --> 00:02:02.389
Under this bridge, that’s all, for the last
20 years we have been lying here."

00:02:02.389 --> 00:02:06.609
Last month, Lallan developed a cough because
of the pollution and cold.

00:02:06.609 --> 00:02:12.830
He earns 10 to 20 rupees – roughly 25 cents
– for every errand he runs, but missing

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:18.080
a day of work means missing crucial wages
to support his family of 8.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:22.640
None of Lallan’s so-called neighbors believe
there is a better alternative.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.860
Some don’t know about the government shelters
– others find them insecure.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.890
His children have grown up under this bridge,
but Lallan still dreams of a better future

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:33.950
for them.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.640
"We don’t want our kids to end up like us.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:45.510
We want them to get good jobs, good homes,
we want that they become good human beings."

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.830
For the coming month, however, the only goal
will be to stay warm.

